title NUL in an expect path

# Both parsers reject this now, in the same words. It spent a round
# in devcheck/pending/ because the reference decoded \000 and
# ACCEPTED it, leaving a real NUL in the argument, while the C side
# rejected it.
#
# What made it worth chasing was not the rarity but the shape: it was
# one implementation giving two answers about one byte depending on
# which field it landed in, since the same parser's LEAF decoder
# already rejected NUL. A parser that disagrees with itself is worse
# than two parsers that disagree with each other, because nothing
# external can catch it.
#
# It announced itself oddly, too: the reference's dump gained a raw
# NUL, so diff stopped printing lines and said "binary files differ".
# A text gate that suddenly says binary is reporting that something
# emitted a byte no text format should hold -- a signal we get for
# free from tools we did not write.
#
# The message quotes nothing, because a string holding a NUL cannot
# be quoted usefully: in C the quoted form would print as a truncated
# fragment and actively misdescribe what was read.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: plain

expect quarantined /a\000b
